<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>💦 LIVE CONDENSATION</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/condensation/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261009-t13z-condensation-anl-g4" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Condensation-favorability index 0-100 (DERIVED from HRRR analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>0 No condensation</b> tan (dew-point depression at/above 2.5 C with RH at/below 60%) &rarr; <b>20 Relative humidity</b> gold &rarr; <b>40 Dew-point depression</b> green &rarr; <b>60 Dew-point depression</b> teal &rarr; <b>80 Surfaces stay wet</b> steel blue &rarr; <b>100 Saturation</b> deep blue (spread at/below 0 C with RH 100%). Relative humidity alone reaches 35; the depression term alone reaches 65; both together reach saturation. Same index always shows the same color — not relative humidity renamed, not dew point renamed, no fog visibility gate.</p><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (derived HRRR analysis), hourly cycles</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km 2 m TMP+DPT+RH analysis, hourly cycles (derived condensation-favorability index)<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE CONDENSATION</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/condensation/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261009-t13z-condensation-anl-g4" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Condensation-favorability index 0-100 (DERIVED from HRRR analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>0 No condensation</b> tan (dew-point depression at/above 2.5 C with RH at/below 60%) &rarr; <b>20 Relative humidity</b> gold &rarr; <b>40 Dew-point depression</b> green &rarr; <b>60 Dew-point depression</b> teal &rarr; <b>80 Surfaces stay wet</b> steel blue &rarr; <b>100 Saturation</b> deep blue (spread at/below 0 C with RH 100%). Relative humidity alone reaches 35; the depression term alone reaches 65; both together reach saturation. Same index always shows the same color — not relative humidity renamed, not dew point renamed, no fog visibility gate.</p><p>A derived condensation-favorability index from operational HRRR temperature, dew point, and humidity analysis: where near-surface air sits closest to saturation, dew, frost, and moisture films form on boats, docks, optics, and shoreline surfaces. High values flag nights and mornings when surfaces stay wet. Derivation: condensation = 100*(0.65*clip((2.5-S)/2.5,0,1)^0.7 + 0.35*clip((RH-60)/40,0,1)), S = TMP-DPT in C deg. Answers how close near-surface air is to saturation (dew/frost/film condensation potential). Uses no wind or visibility data, unlike fog risk; is not relative humidity or dew point itself.</p><p>Condensation science (surface thermodynamics and boundary-layer meteorology) studies the phase change when humid air contacts surfaces at or below the dew point. The dew-point depression (temperature minus dew point) measures the remaining cooling needed; relative humidity weights how broadly saturated the air mass is. Together they predict dew and frost formation relevant to agriculture, infrastructure, and Great Lakes marine operations.</p><p><b>Units:</b> condensation index 0-100 (display; derived, see derivation)<br><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km 2 m TMP+DPT+RH analysis, hourly cycles (derived condensation-favorability index)<br><b>Update:</b> Checked hourly; republishes only on a newer HRRR cycle.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p>]]></description>
      <GroundOverlay>
        <name>💦 LIVE CONDENSATION</name>
